WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:03.280
- [Reporter] Ecuador is
at war with drug gangs.

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:06.060
The South American country
declared what it called

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:08.880
an internal armed conflict after gunmen

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.955
took over a TV studio and
launched a series of attacks.

00:00:12.955 --> 00:00:14.580
(car bangs)
(people screams)

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.340
Fighting intensified
after a top gang leader

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:19.187
escaped from prison on Sunday,

00:00:19.187 --> 00:00:22.830
prompting a 60-day state of emergency.

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:25.645
Here's what's caused
Ecuador's wave of violence

00:00:25.645 --> 00:00:28.740
and what's at stake for
the region and the US

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.390
as gangs fight for control

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:32.959
of lucrative cocaine trafficking routes.

00:00:32.959 --> 00:00:35.520
- Ecuador has been going
to a surge of violence

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.070
over the last three or four years.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:39.750
It's gone from one of the
safest country in Latin America

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:40.869
to one of the most violent,

00:00:40.869 --> 00:00:42.570
and that's due to the drug trade.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:45.039
On Sunday, what happened is
that a lot of the drug gangs,

00:00:45.039 --> 00:00:48.120
their leaders are located in the prisons.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:49.799
And one of the leaders of
one of the biggest gangs

00:00:49.799 --> 00:00:53.160
in Ecuador escaped from prison on Sunday.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.590
That prompted a response
from the new president,

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:57.488
Daniel Noboa, who issued
a state of emergency

00:00:57.488 --> 00:00:59.970
and sent in law enforcement

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:03.180
to try to take control of the prisons.

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.280
A day after that happened, on Tuesday,

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:09.150
another gang members took over
a TV station in Guayaquil,

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:10.020
the country's biggest city.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.760
Then across the country, really,

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:13.451
there was different
incidents that occurred,

00:01:13.451 --> 00:01:14.790
kinda car bombs.

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:15.880
Police were kidnapped.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:18.104
It seemed like a very coordinated response

00:01:18.104 --> 00:01:20.523
to the government to
try to send a message.

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:22.593
President Daniel Noboa,

00:01:22.593 --> 00:01:24.810
who's only been in office since November,

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:27.960
he declared an internal
armed conflict in Ecuador.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:30.690
So what that practically
means is that the military

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:34.380
will now have a greater role
in trying to improve security.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.930
So basically, the gangs
become military targets.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:40.050
The soldiers are able to
try to neutralize them.

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:41.197
The root of this wave of violence

00:01:41.197 --> 00:01:43.860
is an increasing drug trade.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.900
Drugs flowing in from Colombia, mainly,

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.330
that are going up to Europe
and the United States.

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:50.160
They are now moving into Ecuador.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.062
A lot of the cocaine
production in Colombia

00:01:52.062 --> 00:01:53.940
is on the border with Ecuador.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:56.520
The security experts say
that it's actually easier

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.910
for Colombian gangs to
move the drugs into Ecuador

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:01.182
rather than straight outta Colombia.

00:02:01.182 --> 00:02:02.807
Ecuador has really good roads,

00:02:02.807 --> 00:02:04.328
has really good infrastructure,

00:02:04.328 --> 00:02:06.600
has little security at the ports.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.150
Ecuador also is a dollarized economy.

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:12.362
So that makes it easier for drug gangs

00:02:12.362 --> 00:02:14.850
to launder their money into the country.

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:17.340
And that has attracted a lot of attention

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:19.310
from international
cartels, Mexican cartels,

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:21.115
Albanian cartels, for example,

00:02:21.115 --> 00:02:23.130
and it has provided a lot more resources

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:25.890
for the local gangs who
are now controlling this.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:29.070
At the same time, a really
key factor in this violence

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:32.076
has been the government's
inability to control

00:02:32.076 --> 00:02:34.170
what happens inside the prisons.

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:36.087
The prisons have become the headquarters

00:02:36.087 --> 00:02:38.640
and the de facto
headquarters for the gangs

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:42.503
where they operate with
immunity from inside.

00:02:42.503 --> 00:02:45.510
Security experts will say
that what needs to be done,

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:46.560
the first thing needs to be done,

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.270
is that the government
needs to take back control

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:49.140
of the prisons.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:53.970
Without that, it's very
difficult to weaken the gangs

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:56.250
and to reduce the violence.

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:58.200
Longer term, I mean, it's an issue

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.170
of how to deal with the
international drug trade.

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:05.250
And then also the US, Europe, Colombia,

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:07.950
Mexico, several American
countries right now

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:10.050
are struggling with increase to crime.

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:11.100
Ecuador is, I think,

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:14.943
the most startling example of that by far.

